## Why This Is Asked
Validates practical testing of optimistic UI patterns and error handling in a beginner-friendly React scenario.

## Key Concepts
- Optimistic UI updates
- Rollback on API failure
- Mock service worker (MSW) for deterministic tests
- Unit vs integration tests in React
- CI reliability with simulated network faults

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example test skeleton using React Testing Library and MSW
import { render, screen, fireEvent, waitFor } from '@testing-library/react';
import { rest } from 'msw';
import { setupServer } from 'msw/node';
import TaskList from './TaskList';

const server = setupServer(
  rest.get('/api/tasks', (req, res, ctx) => {
    return res(ctx.json([{ id:1, text:'Task A'},{id:2, text:'Task B'}]));
  }),
  rest.post('/api/tasks', (req, res, ctx) => {
    return res(ctx.status(201), ctx.json({ id:3, text: req.body.text }));
  })
);

beforeAll(() => server.listen());
afterEach(() => server.resetHandlers());
afterAll(() => server.close());

// tests would render TaskList and simulate add, asserting optimistic update and final state
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend tests for concurrent adds?
- How would you test timeouts and retry logic?

## Why This Question Is Asked
This evaluates your ability to validate a production-grade streaming ML pipeline under realistic conditions, covering data integrity, performance under load, drift handling, and safe deployment strategies.

## Key Concepts
- End-to-end streaming test strategies and synthetic data generation
- Backpressure handling and consumer lag in Kafka/Flink
- Feature drift detection and model retraining triggers
- Canary rollouts, shadow traffic, and safe rollback procedures

## Code Example
```javascript
// End-to-end test harness wiring synthetic replay, latency tracking,
// and assertion validation
async function runE2ETest(config) {
  const syntheticData = generateLabeledAnomalies(config);
  const results = await pipeline.process(syntheticData);

  const latency = measureLatency(results);
  assert(latency.p95 < 300, 'P95 latency exceeded budget');
  assert(latency.p99 < 1000, 'P99 latency exceeded budget');

  const correctness = validateChecksums(results);
  assert(correctness, 'Data correctness check failed');

  return { latency, correctness };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Real-time collaboration applications face significant timing and consistency challenges across distributed services. This question evaluates your ability to design practical testing strategies that verify message ordering, presence accuracy, and read receipt functionality under realistic load conditions and network failures, with emphasis on concrete data validation and automated integration testing.

## Key Concepts

- End-to-end message delivery guarantees and in-order semantics
- Presence tracking accuracy during network partitions and recovery
- Cross-shard consistency for read receipts and typing indicators
- Data-driven testing approaches with realistic user behavior patterns
- Automated validation integrated into CI/CD pipelines

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-test harness sketch for in-order delivery verification
let lastSeq = {};
const validateOrder = (roomId, message) => {
  if (lastSeq[roomId] >= message.sequenceId) {
    throw new Error(`Out-of-order message in room ${roomId}`);
  }
  lastSeq[roomId] = message.sequenceId;
};
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests for queue workers and external dependencies are common and error-prone. This question probes practical thinking on idempotency and retry strategies.

## Key Concepts
- Idempotent processing with dedup keys
- Retry/backoff for transient failures (429, 5xx)
- Integration tests with mocks for external SMTP
- Deterministic fixtures and CI validation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example Jest test sketch (pseudo-code)
const { processMessage } = require('./notifier');
const queue = require('./fakeQueue');
const smtp = require('./fakeSmtp');

test('idempotent processing with same messageId', async () => {
  const msg = { id: 'm1', to: 'a@example.com', body: 'hi' };
  await queue.publish(msg);
  await processMessage(msg);
  await processMessage(msg); // duplicate
  expect(smtp.sentCount).toBe(1);
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you simulate SMTP latency and network flakiness in CI?
- How would you extend tests to guarantee exactly-once semantics across restarts?

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates your ability to design practical testing strategies for common API patterns involving user signup, rate limiting, and idempotency. It tests understanding of testing methodologies, external service mocking, and CI/CD considerations.

## Key Concepts
- Input validation testing
- Idempotency key patterns
- Rate limiting verification
- External service mocking strategies
- Retry mechanisms and failure simulation
- CI/CD integration testing

## Code Example
```javascript
// Idempotency implementation sketch
function signup(req, res, db, emailService) {
  const idempotencyKey = req.headers['Idempotency-Key'];

  // Check for existing request
  const existingResult = await db.getResult(idempotencyKey);
  if (existingResult) {
    return res.status(200).json(existingResult);
  }

  // Validate input and create user
  const user = await validateAndCreateUser(req.body);

  // Send email with retry logic
  try {
    await emailService.sendVerification(user.email);
  } catch (error) {
    // Handle email failure with retry queue
  }

  // Store result with idempotency key
  const result = { userId: user.id, status: 'created' };
  await db.storeResult(idempotencyKey, result);

  return res.status(201).json(result);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt the testing strategy for a microservices architecture with distributed signup services?
- What considerations would you add for testing GDPR compliance and data privacy requirements?

